From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DarekM <149845949+aninmucek@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:24:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: s2mps11: Let the S2MPG14 touch supplies set their
 voltage

ABL can leave LDO4M at another voltage than the touch controller needs:
on a Pixel 8a it leaves 3.3 V, read back through the driver's own
get_voltage_sel, while the stock phone runs the Goodix GT9895 there at
3.0 V (its live device tree). With voltage selectors firmware-owned, a
board that fixes 3.0 V fails to probe ("failed to apply
3000000-3000000uV constraint: -EINVAL"), and one without a constraint
would power the controller at ABL's 3.3 V.

Give LDO4M and LDO25M a set_voltage_sel through the regmap, which goes
to the PMIC through ACPM as enable does. The selector tables are
Google's: s2mpg14-regulator.c puts LDO4M in voltage group 5 (1.8 V
minimum) and LDO25M in group 4 (0.7 V), each with 64 steps of 25 mV
(s2mpg14-register.h), and writes the selector into the low six bits of
the control register (0x2e and 0x43), as the descriptors here already
read it. The other S2MPG14 outputs keep firmware-owned selectors.

The core calls the setter only to bring a voltage into the board's
constraints, so a board without voltage constraints (zumapro-tegu), or
whose constraint already matches what ABL left (zuma-husky's fixed
3.3 V, which probes today without a setter), sees no write.

Tested on a Pixel 8a from slot B (7.3.0-rc6), with LDO4M fixed at
3.0 V and LDO25M at 1.8 V in the board file and nothing consuming them:
the core logs "touch-avdd: Bringing 3300000uV into 3000000-3000000uV";
both rails then read back 3000000 and 1800000 uV (the PMIC regmap has
no cache, so that is the register through ACPM), disabled, with no
users, and stay so past the unused-regulator cleanup. Nothing enabled
them in that boot.

Link: https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/google-modules/soc/gs/+/204078758737776959ecc85c527a00fd2cabb62f/drivers/regulator/s2mpg14-regulator.c
Link: https://android.googlesource.com/kernel/google-modules/soc/gs/+/204078758737776959ecc85c527a00fd2cabb62f/include/linux/mfd/samsung/s2mpg14-register.h
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: DarekM <149845949+aninmucek@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/regulator/s2mps11.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/s2mps11.c b/drivers/regulator/s2mps11.c
index 647b46c75..2839998df 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/s2mps11.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/s2mps11.c
@@ -2282,13 +2282,30 @@ static const struct regulator_ops s2mpg14_ldo_ops = {
 	.disable		= regulator_disable_regmap,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The touchscreen supplies can also be set: ABL may leave them at another
+ * voltage than the board's controller needs (3.3 V on LDO4M where the
+ * Pixel 8a's runs at 3.0 V). The selector tables are Google's
+ * (s2mpg14-regulator.c: LDO4M group 5, LDO25M group 4, 64 steps of 25 mV)
+ * and set the low six bits of the control register, as there.
+ */
+static const struct regulator_ops s2mpg14_ldo_vsel_ops = {
+	.list_voltage		= regulator_list_voltage_linear,
+	.map_voltage		= regulator_map_voltage_linear,
+	.get_voltage_sel	= regulator_get_voltage_sel_regmap,
+	.set_voltage_sel	= regulator_set_voltage_sel_regmap,
+	.is_enabled		= regulator_is_enabled_regmap,
+	.enable			= regulator_enable_regmap,
+	.disable		= regulator_disable_regmap,
+};
+
 static const struct regulator_desc s2mpg14_regulators[] = {
 	{
 		.name = "ldo4m",
 		.id = 3,
 		.of_match = of_match_ptr("ldo4m"),
 		.regulators_node = of_match_ptr("regulators"),
-		.ops = &s2mpg14_ldo_ops,
+		.ops = &s2mpg14_ldo_vsel_ops,
 		.type = REGULATOR_VOLTAGE,
 		.owner = THIS_MODULE,
 		.min_uV = 1800000,
@@ -2352,7 +2369,7 @@ static const struct regulator_desc s2mpg14_regulators[] = {
 		.id = 24,
 		.of_match = of_match_ptr("ldo25m"),
 		.regulators_node = of_match_ptr("regulators"),
-		.ops = &s2mpg14_ldo_ops,
+		.ops = &s2mpg14_ldo_vsel_ops,
 		.type = REGULATOR_VOLTAGE,
 		.owner = THIS_MODULE,
 		.min_uV = 700000,
